page('Programming Finance<br/>Project Report #1')
p('<b>Conceptual design | Fall 2026 | October 18, 2026</b>')
p('Submission metadata: team name and member names have not been supplied. Add these before submission. This report describes the proposed system; implementation and verification are documented separately.')
h('Purpose and intended users')
p('The proposed server-side application helps individuals with modest investing knowledge compare explainable stock strategies, manage a simulated portfolio of approximately 20 companies, and understand how coordinated trades change portfolio risk. It supports investment horizons of 1, 2, and 5 years. Its emphasis is software engineering and transparent evaluation, rather than a claim of profitable investment advice.')
h('Questions the system answers')
p('What do historical price trends suggest for my portfolio? How often should I review its allocation? Which combination of purchases and sales would reduce estimated variance within a chosen historical window? What happened after a recorded recommendation? Can my simulated orders be aggregated with orders from other users without sharing their cash?')
h('Scope and design basis')
p('The supplied four-page assignment is the authoritative course specification. This submission addresses conceptual design; it does not require or present program code. The supplied screenshots of Lecture #1 slides 13 and 17 inform the concepts and stock actions below. Only these two slides, not the entire lecture deck, were reviewed. Established references appear on page 8.')
p('The proposed server will use MySQL for persistent state. Console output is sufficient for the required scope; coding and test results belong to later reports. An optional explanatory dashboard presents the same services through Overview, Strategy, Trades, Baskets, and Performance views. There is no real brokerage connection, margin, short selling, or real-money execution. Basket trading is adopted as the optional assignment enhancement requested in the user prompt.')
page('1 | A minimal financial vocabulary')
p('Five familiar concepts form the user-facing model. Investment horizon is an attribute of a portfolio; holdings and cash are portfolio state. Technical analysis and risk are measurements used by a strategy, rather than extra accounts or instruments users must manage.')
h('Stock')
p('A stock represents an ownership interest in a company. The application identifies each stock by a symbol and uses dated prices to measure changes in value; holding a stock does not guarantee a positive return. [1]')
h('Portfolio')
p('A portfolio is the collection of investments held for an investor. Here it contains stock positions and simulated cash, with a chosen time horizon; allocation describes how its value is distributed among those positions. [2]')
h('Trading strategy')
p('A trading strategy is a repeatable set of rules for deciding when and how to trade. This system uses technical indicators derived from historical prices to make its rules inspectable, while recognizing that those indicators cannot establish future performance. [3]')
h('Order')
p('An order is an instruction to buy or sell a specified security. Market orders seek execution at an available market price, but an observed quote does not guarantee the price available at execution; this project instead uses an explicitly labeled price-taking simulator. [4]')
h('Basket trade')
p('A basket trade coordinates multiple securities as a group. The project extends that idea with an operational aggregation batch that groups compatible simulated orders from several users while retaining separate beneficial ownership. [5]')
h('Why these five are sufficient')
p('Stocks identify what can be traded; portfolios identify ownership, resources, and goals; strategies explain recommendations; orders express user decisions; baskets coordinate them. Quotes, recommendations, fills, and evaluations are supporting records of these concepts. User identity, data providers, AI adapters, and database transactions are implementation services, not additional financial concepts.')
h('Connection to Lecture #1, slide 13')
p('Slide 13 lists portfolios, positions, orders, strategies, price history and risk among the candidate concepts. We retain portfolios, orders and strategies; represent positions as holdings and price history as stock state; and treat volatility and correlation as strategy measurements. Investor/trader roles share one user identity. Brokerage, PayPal funding, dividends and tax accounting are outside this simulator; watchlists are not needed for the selected universe.')
page('2 | Essential state and operations')
table([['Concept','Minimum state','Operations and purpose'],['Stock','Symbol; currency; dated raw quotes; historical closes with adjustment status; source and coverage','Inspect quote/history; buy or sell via an order; hold by retaining shares. Source and time make evidence auditable.'],['Portfolio','Owner; cash; whole-share holdings; horizon (1/2/5 years)','Fund simulation; inspect value; choose horizon; request analysis; approve rebalance. Ownership separates users.'],['Trading strategy','Indicator windows; review interval; allocation constraints; cutoff; recorded recommendation','Analyze; propose target weights and trades; evaluate later outcomes. Cutoff prevents future data entering a decision.'],['Order','Owner portfolio; symbol; buy/sell; integer quantity; state; request key; timestamps','Submit; cancel while pending; execute or reject. Stable keys prevent duplicate fills after retries.'],['Basket trade','Batch key; cutoff; member orders; symbol/side groups; fills and allocations','Collect eligible orders; validate; execute; reconcile. Every allocation retains its original owner.']],[76,190,238])
h('Why this state is needed')
p('Symbols and currency identify the asset and price unit; dated history supports analysis. Cash and share quantities bound affordable trades; the owner prevents mixing investors; the horizon sets the goal. Strategy windows and constraints define repeatable advice, while the review interval says when to reconsider it. Order side, quantity and status specify intent and progress. Basket membership and allocations preserve individual entitlements. References [1]-[5] and lecture slides [7] motivate these choices; timestamps, request keys and batch keys are supporting audit safeguards, not extra financial concepts.')
h('Derived records and invariants')

p('Cash and holdings must remain nonnegative. No sale can exceed owned shares, and no purchase can spend another user\'s money. A committed fill updates cash, shares, order status, and audit records together; an aborted transaction changes none of them.')
h('Stock actions from Lecture #1, slide 17')
p('Buy(symbol, quantity, portfolio) creates a purchase order; a committed fill reduces virtual cash and increases shares. Sell uses the same inputs and reverses these changes within owned-share limits. Hold(symbol) means keeping the position unchanged, so it requires no execution. The lecture example uses a payment/deposit account; this design uses portfolio cash rather than PayPal. Unlike its execution-only option, our strategy service also provides advice for explicit user review.')

