p('<b>Cardinalities.</b> One user owns many portfolios. Each portfolio holds zero or more stocks and can submit many orders; each order refers to exactly one stock and one portfolio. A basket contains multiple orders, each with a traceable fill allocation. A strategy produces many dated recommendations for portfolios.')
h('Analysis does not execute')
p('Requesting analysis reads a portfolio and historical observations, computes indicators and covariance, and stores a recommendation. It does not change cash or holdings. The user must explicitly approve a proposed trade set. Optional AI text explains computed results and is labeled separately from numeric evidence.')
h('Execution changes related concepts atomically')
p('Approving a coordinated rebalance validates all legs at one quote snapshot, sells first within the transaction, then buys using only that portfolio\'s cash. Either all legs commit or all roll back. The proposal is revalidated because holdings and prices may have changed since analysis.')
h('Aggregation is distinct from portfolio coordination')
p('A multi-user batch groups pending orders by symbol and side. It uses one price per group and full fills only, preserving order-level allocations; buys and sells are not netted. An unaffordable order is rejected without borrowing from another portfolio. Cancellation and execution compete under locks, so an order cannot both cancel and fill.')
page('4 | Concrete use cases')
table([['Use case','Interaction and observable result'],['Set a goal','A user creates a portfolio, funds simulated cash, and chooses 1, 2, or 5 years. Invalid horizons are rejected.'],['Inspect evidence','The user requests history for up to 20 years. The response shows available coverage, source, and missing history; recent IPOs are not given invented observations.'],['Ask for a strategy','For 20 selected stocks, the service reports trends, RSI, return and volatility over stated intervals. It proposes monthly, quarterly, or semiannual reviews according to horizon.'],['Reduce estimated risk','The user compares existing, equal-weight, and constrained minimum-variance allocations using the same aligned observations. The system reports infeasibility or insufficient shared history explicitly.'],['Approve coupled trades','The user approves whole-share sell and buy legs. Execution rechecks current cash and quotes; residual cash and each fill are displayed. Failure rolls back the complete rebalance.'],['Join a batch','Two users submit buys of the same stock. A simulator batch prices them together, allocates each entire order, and records separate cash debits and holdings.'],['Cancel or reject','A pending order may be cancelled. Stale quotes, insufficient cash, and insufficient shares produce explicit errors or recorded rejections without inconsistent balances.'],['Evaluate a decision','The user requests performance after the recommendation cutoff. The service excludes earlier observations and reports measured outcomes alongside baselines and assumptions.']],[108,396])
h('An independently checkable example')
p('At a simulated quote of $12.50, an account with $100 buys 3 shares: cost $37.50, ending cash $62.50, and holdings increase by 3. A second user buying 2 shares pays $25.00 from their own cash. The aggregate is 5 shares and $62.50, exactly equal to the two allocations; repeating the same request must not debit either user twice.')
page('5 | Analysis, integrations, and boundaries')
h('Explainable analysis')
p('The initial strategy uses moving averages for trend, 14-observation RSI for momentum, and historical return, volatility, correlation, and drawdown for context. It compares trailing 1-, 2-, 5-, 10-, and 20-year intervals when available. The future investment horizon is distinct from these past observation windows.')
p('A long-only minimum-variance proposal uses aligned returns, a covariance estimate, and a concentration cap. It compares estimated risk against existing holdings and equal weights over the identical window. Constraints, sample size, regularization, and convergence are reported. Diversification can reduce some risks but cannot guarantee protection against losses. [2]')
h('Small example: buy, sell or hold')
p('At a scheduled review for the 1-year strategy, compare the 20-observation and 100-observation simple moving averages (SMA). Suppose the fast SMA is $105 and the slow SMA is $100. The trend rule favors owning the stock: if no shares are held, this is a buy signal; if already invested, hold. If the fast SMA later falls to $98 while the slow SMA is $100, the rule favors cash: sell an existing position, or remain in cash if none is held. These are illustrative numbers, not actual market observations.')
p('This is the separate SMA timing rule, not the multi-stock optimizer. Its signals use only observations known at review time; replay executes no earlier than the next observation. RSI provides context. In the main portfolio proposal, target shares above, below or equal to current shares imply buy, sell or hold respectively. Users approve trades explicitly; a signal alone never moves money.')
h('Risk optimization assumptions')
p('Risk means estimated variance of aligned historical daily returns, not the probability of loss. The model assumes the selected historical window is informative about future co-movement, although correlations can change. It uses long-only target weights summing to 100%, with no leverage or short selling and a 20% maximum per company. At least five companies are therefore required; cash is treated as zero-return and zero-risk when comparing the existing portfolio.')
p('The covariance estimate requires at least 61 shared prices; a small diagonal adjustment (1e-8) improves numerical stability. No reliable improvement is promised when data are insufficient or constraints cannot be met. The continuous allocation ignores taxes, liquidity and trading costs; whole-share conversion can leave cash and change the realized weights. Lower estimated historical variance is not guaranteed future risk reduction or a forecast of higher returns.')
page('6 | Data coverage and evaluation boundaries')
h('External information and AI boundary')
p('Replaceable adapters separate historical analysis from current quotes and news. We plan to analyze up to 20 years of historical prices, subject to provider access and actual availability. The selected setup uses 20 supplied Nasdaq-format CSVs and Finnhub for quotes/news. File validation confirms 2,513 observations per company (50,260 total), from October 4, 2016 to October 2, 2026: about 10 years, not 20. The adjustment method is unverified. Full 20-year real coverage has not been established; shorter intervals must be labeled with their actual dates. Alpha Vantage remains an optional adjusted-history adapter requiring appropriate entitlement. [6]')
p('An offline provider generates deterministic synthetic prices solely for tests and demonstrations. It never claims to be actual market history. News and AI interfaces are optional and credential-based; external text is untrusted. AI may produce a separate explanation but cannot change calculated signals or authorize orders.')
h('Evaluation and limitations')
p('Historical evaluation makes decisions from observations available at time T and executes no earlier than the next observation. Forward evaluation records a decision first and waits for later data. Both must identify timing, costs, slippage, benchmarks, and coverage. Historical replay is not evidence of future live success.')
p('Strict execution accepts quotes up to five minutes old. Explicit demo mode accepts Finnhub quotes up to seven days old for hypothetical fills, preserving the observation time. USD equities, whole shares, zero execution fees, and full fills keep scope manageable. The simulator does not reproduce live liquidity, settlement, corporate-action processing on held shares, or tax accounting. Adjusted data may be revised; point-in-time corporate-action history is outside this prototype, so retrospective results require that caveat.')
